hi there steve kaufman  today i want to talk about reading the  importance of reading and just how  powerful it is  as a way of learning languages as a way  of learning about things in general  reading is tremendously powerful  now there are people who say  you know i don't like to read  i like to watch movies  and i have never considered watching  movies  as a great way to learn languages  to me watching movies is either a reward  for having learned the language  or um a stimulus  so i'll give you two examples  my wife likes to watch these  different language  movies and series on netflix  and so she got us  watching this thing called gran hotel  grand hotel which is a spanish series  and i'll now be sort of addicted to it  we watch it every night  but it's in spanish and so it's  tremendously enjoyable for me to watch  this movie and understand the spanish  that's a reward  uh if movies come on television at home  and typically in canada they're in the  original version if it's in german if  it's in chinese if it's in japanese it's  tremendously rewarding to be able to  watch the movie in the language  um  so uh but it's also a stimulus when i  started with russian i remember watching  this movie jestoki romance which was a  uh  story out of the 19th century  it's actually a play  called  without a dowry  by um ostrowski but it was so romantic  it was so out of the 19th century russia  far away wow you know i want to learn  that language so movies you know it's  fun it stimulates you it's a reward but  it's i can't learn from that i i learn  so much more from reading and of course  from listening because i read i can just  i'm sitting on a plane i open a book i'm  uh you know there are so many situations  and the words then you know reading is  so word rich you have to connect  words to meaning there's no picture  there's nothing to explain  or to show you what's happening you have  to be able to imagine that from the  words so to me reading is is  tremendously powerful that's why with my  arabic for example  you know i could have decided to go with  a phonetic script and try to get to  where i can say some things right away  hello how are you what's your name  and i can't do that in russian excuse me  in arabic but i am determined to become  a reader of arabic because  reading is a way that i can continue to  improve my knowledge  in the language  i can read online i can import things  into link and read  whereas my ability to find people to  speak to in arabic is going to be  limited so reading to me is is extremely  important and i'm always willing to  invest the time and i also recommend to  people invest the time  uh  in learning the script whether it be the  cyrillic alphabet for russian or  ukrainian  or uh you know chinese characters or or  you know the japanese you know hiragana  or hangul  uh or the greek or whatever it might be  in arabic i want to learn the script so  that i can read so that i will have this  ability at any time  to pick up something and read it and be  constantly improving in the language now  some people say well i can read but i  can't speak well you know to speak you  have to speak so at some point you have  to speak and you have to speak a lot but  many of the people who have said to me  you know i can read but i have trouble  speaking in fact they don't read very  much so i say well what do you read in  english like i've seen this with  immigrants they say well you know i can  read that's not my problem i can't speak  and i say what do you read  for example have you ever read a novel  in english have you read a novel from  you know the the beginning to the end  no  okay so if you read the odd newspaper  article that's not really reading i want  to get to where i have read novels in  polish in russian  i haven't yet done so in greek i never  reached that level but i want to get to  that level i want to get to uh you know  on our link count 30 40  50 000 known word level so that i can  pick up books and read them from cover  to cover because that is really going to  bring the language  into me  now there are people who say  i'm elitist  uh you know  because lots of people learn languages  and can't read you know  you know  illiterate societies where people speak  two or three or four languages well sure  that's true  um  of course that's true  there was a period  when we had no writing i mean homer  the great  you know  barred writer of ulis you know the  odyssey and uh  you know the battle of troy and all this  kind of stuff  the iliad  uh in those days we had this wonderful  oral tradition the great  um  you know ramayana  uh um stories in in  southeast asia and so forth these are  all oral traditions yeah but in the  meantime we invented writing  uh writing is a way of recording  speech just like a  tape recorder or an mp3 player records  speech so we use these uh  recording devices in order to carry  sound with us listening is very powerful  by the same token we have developed  writing so why wouldn't we use it why  would we say no no i don't want to read  i just want to rely on  on you know hearing it  again people do what they want to do but  to me  reading and stephen crash is very strong  on this he feels that  if we just allowed and encourage people  to read kids in school they would learn  better their language skills would  improve more  than any deliberate instruction and  explanation of grammar and so forth and  so on because we can consume so much  uh  by by reading and this is all training  our brain in the language whether it be  our own language i mean obviously  those people who express themselves the  best in their own language are typically  people who read a lot if you want to  improve your ability to express yourself  to write  if you want to write better you have to  read more and read material that is well  written so  reading is tremendously convenient it's  a powerful way  to learn and we have to train ourselves  and and you know obviously like if i  watch the uh spanish movie and my wife  likes to have the english subtitles on i  have no trouble following the english  subtitles  even in span if they had spanish  subtitles which would be better in my  opinion from a language learning  perspective i would be able to keep up  because it's the same script however if  i wanted if i watch a chinese movie and  they have chinese subtitles i have real  trouble keeping up uh similarly with  cyrillic or any uh you know writing  system that we have had less exposure to  so the key there is the more you expose  yourself to a writing system the more  you train the brain in reading the  better you're going to read there's a  constant gradual  not noticeable but nevertheless gradual  improvement so my advice to people is in  all of your various language learning  activities make sure you do enough  reading it's a very powerful way to  improve in any language  and uh some people may want to spend  their time watching tv and and listening  to music i don't think in the long run  that's going to take you as far as  committing to doing a lot of reading bye  for now  [Music]  you

